---
/* WHAT RUNS EACH JOB

   Three objects: a lane picker, one profile that mixes three lanes, and two
   cards for the questions the lanes cannot answer.

   ── WHY THE LANES ARE A PICKER AND NOT FOUR CARDS ──────────────────────────

   They were four cards side by side: what the lane is, who runs on it, what
   credential it costs, how many of the eight jobs it serves. All true, all
   readable, and between them they named four model ids out of the thirty-five
   in the file. A reader deciding between lanes could read the entire section
   and still not know what would run their dictation.

   Before the cards there was a grid -- eight jobs by three lanes, twenty-four
   model ids, and it was the worst surface on the page for the reason the
   catalogue module's header records: what a reader takes from a wall of mono
   strings is "there are a lot of models", which is not the claim, and the four
   `none` cells were the only actionable thing on it and its quietest text.

   THE PICKER IS THE SAME VALUES WITH THE READER'S HAND ON THEM. One lane's
   eight jobs at a time, and the reader opened that lane. That is the whole
   difference and it is not cosmetic: a matrix has to be compared with itself
   before it says anything, while a column somebody chose is an answer to the
   question they asked by choosing. The `none` rows stop being the quietest
   text on a grid and become the visible shape of the lane you just opened.

   AND IT IS FOUR RADIOS AND `:checked ~`, NOT AN ISLAND. The whole section
   still ships no JavaScript. A radio group is what a one-of-four choice IS,
   which means the keyboard works, the labels are hit targets, the state
   survives with script disabled, and a screen reader is told it is a choice
   rather than being handed four buttons that mysteriously change a panel.

   ── AND WHY THERE ARE TWO CARDS UNDER IT ───────────────────────────────────

   Two questions decide whether somebody downloads a dictation app, and until
   now this page answered neither. The first is whether it speaks their
   language. The second is whether it works with the network off. Both are
   properties of the engine rather than of any one lane, which is why they sit
   under the picker instead of inside it.

   Every value is read at build time. Lanes, jobs, models, vendors and download
   sizes come from ../lib/catalogue, which reads shared/model_catalogue.json;
   the two language numbers come from ../lib/languages, which reads that same
   file and `src-tauri/src/core/language_detect.rs` and throws rather than
   guessing. Vendor marks come from ../lib/marks. */
import { COUNTS, JOBS, LANE_CARDS, LOCAL_INSTALL, PROFILE } from '../lib/catalogue';
import Mark from './Mark.astro';
import { DETECTED, RECOGNISED } from '../lib/languages';
import { LANGS, MORE } from '../lib/flags';
---

<section class="sec" id="engines">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec__head rise">
      <h2>Nothing runs on one engine.</h2>
      <p>A profile decides what handles each job, and the jobs do not have to agree. Cleanup
        on a small local model, the agent on a frontier one, dictation wherever it is fastest
        that week. Four lanes, and you pick by what it costs you to run rather than by
        whichever model is fashionable this month.</p>
    </div>

    {/* THE PICKER. The radios live here rather than inside the tab list so
        that `:checked ~ .lanes` can reach the panels: a sibling combinator
        only walks forward, so the inputs have to come first in the DOM. They
        are visually hidden, never `display:none` -- a hidden input is out of
        the tab order and takes no keyboard, which would leave the labels as
        the only way in and the arrow keys dead. */}
    <div class="pick rise" data-d="1">
      {LANE_CARDS.map((lane, i) => (
        <input
          class="pick__r"
          type="radio"
          name="lane"
          id={`lane-${lane.key}`}
          checked={i === 0}
        />
      ))}

      <div class="pick__tabs" role="presentation">
        {LANE_CARDS.map((lane) => (
          <label class="pick__t" for={`lane-${lane.key}`}>
            <b>{lane.label}</b>
            <span class="mono">{lane.jobs} of 8</span>
          </label>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="lanes">
        {LANE_CARDS.map((lane) => (
          <div class="lane" data-lane={lane.key}>
            <div class="lane__top">
              <p class="lane__what">{lane.what}</p>
              <p class="lane__cred mono"><b>needs</b> {lane.credential}</p>
            </div>

            {lane.typed ? (
              /* NO ROWS, AND THE ABSENCE IS THE LANE. The server is the
                 reader's and its model list belongs to whoever runs it, so
                 eight rows of model ids here would be asserting a
                 compatibility matrix nobody has measured -- and eight rows of
                 the SAME sentence would be a wall that says less than the
                 sentence does once. What it has instead is the two fields it
                 actually takes, which is `src/screens/data.ts`'s own sentence
                 standing where a model id would. */
              <div class="lane__typed">
                <p class="lane__field mono">
                  <span>https://your-host:8000/v1</span>
                  <span>typed on the endpoint</span>
                </p>
                <p class="lane__note">Every one of the eight, on whatever that server serves.
                  The model list is yours, so this page does not have one.</p>
                {/* THE AXIS ITSELF, WHERE THE PER-JOB ANSWER CANNOT EXIST. */}
                <p class="lane__eight"><b>all eight</b>{
                  JOBS.map((j) => <i>{j.label}</i>)
                }</p>
              </div>
            ) : (
              <ol class="jobs">
                {lane.rows.map((r) => (
                  <li class="job" data-none={r.none ? '1' : undefined}>
                    <span class="job__n">
                      <b>{r.job}</b>
                      <span class="job__w">{r.what}</span>
                    </span>
                    {r.none ? (
                      /* THE ROWS THAT DO NOT RUN ARE THE POINT OF THE LANE.
                         On the grid this replaced they read `none` in the
                         quietest type on the surface; here they are the shape
                         of the column the reader just opened. The card's own
                         sentence above says why. */
                      <span class="job__none mono">not on this lane</span>
                    ) : (
                      <span class="job__m">
                        <Mark v={r.vendor} />
                        <span class="job__id mono">{r.model}</span>
                        {r.size && <span class="job__size mono">{r.size}</span>}
                      </span>
                    )}
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ol>
            )}

            {!lane.typed && (
              <p class="lane__marks">
                {lane.vendors.map((v) => (
                  <span>
                    <Mark v={v} />
                    <span>{v.label}</span>
                  </span>
                ))}
              </p>
            )}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    {/* THE CLAIM, PERFORMED ONCE. Four jobs on three different lanes, and
        every model on it is the catalogue's own default for that pair -- so
        this is not an illustration of what a profile could look like, it is
        what the runtime would do if you set these four. */}
    <div class="prof rise lit" data-d="1">
      <div class="prof__head">
        <b class="prof__t">One profile, and the jobs disagree on purpose</b>
        <span class="prof__sub">four of the eight, set to three different lanes</span>
      </div>
      <ol class="prof__rows">
        {PROFILE.map((r) => (
          <li class="prof__r">
            <span class="prof__job">
              <b>{r.job}</b>
              <span class="prof__jw">{r.what}</span>
            </span>
            <span class="prof__lane mono">{r.lane}</span>
            <span class="prof__v">
              <Mark v={r.vendor} />
              <span class="prof__m mono">{r.model}</span>
            </span>
            <span class="prof__why">{r.why}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="eng__two rise" data-d="2">
      {/* TWO NUMBERS, AND THEY ARE DIFFERENT NUMBERS ON PURPOSE.

          Every product in this category prints one language figure and none of
          them says what it measures. These are two, because the questions are
          two: what the recogniser can TRANSCRIBE, which is a property of
          Whisper and comes off the catalogue rows that state it, and what
          WordScript can NAME afterwards, which is the detector's own table and
          is smaller.

          A THIRD ROW WAS HERE AND IT IS GONE. It read `0 guessed`, with the
          word floor under it, and the argument for it was that a detector
          which refuses is the one claim on this page a competitor would have
          to change their product to match. Reported on reading it: nobody
          arrives at this card wondering whether the product guesses, so a card
          that answers it is answering an accusation nobody made -- and a
          product that volunteers what it does not do reads as a product with
          something to be defensive about. The refusal is still the behaviour
          and it is still documented where it belongs, in ADR 0180 and in
          `core/language_detect.rs`. It is not a selling point, and printing it
          as one cost the card its credibility rather than earning it. */}
      <div class="card lang">
        <h3>Switch language mid-paragraph.</h3>
        <p class="lang__p">Nothing has to be set first and nothing has to be set back. The
          language is read off the text that came back, not off a dropdown and not from the
          provider &mdash; on the two lanes most dictations go through, no provider reports
          one.</p>
        <dl class="lang__f mono">
          <div>
            <dt>{RECOGNISED}+</dt>
            <dd>languages the recogniser transcribes</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>{DETECTED}</dt>
            <dd>it names in your own record, so the app can count what you actually
              dictate in</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        {/* SEVENTEEN OF THE SEVENTY, EACH IN ITS OWN SCRIPT.

            The flag is a convention and the name beside it is the answer --
            ../lib/flags argues that out, including why the two languages the
            detector names that have no country are not on this row. Every
            name is an endonym read from ICU at build time, so what a chip says
            is what a speaker calls their own language.

            The count at the end is subtracted rather than typed: adding a chip
            takes one off it without anyone having to remember. */}
        <ul class="lang__c">
          {LANGS.map((l) => (
            <li>
              <svg class="fl" viewBox={l.viewBox} aria-hidden="true" set:html={l.body} />
              <span lang={l.code}>{l.name}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
          <li class="lang__more mono">+{MORE} more</li>
        </ul>

      </div>

      {/* THE OFFLINE CARD SAYS ONE WORD THE PAGE HAS NEVER SAID.

          The Local lane's credential line has always read "none, by
          construction", which is the fact -- but a reader scanning for whether
          this thing works on a train is scanning for the word "offline", and
          the word was nowhere on the page. It is not a fifth lane and not a
          mode; it is what the Local lane already is, said in the words
          somebody searches with. */}
      <div class="card off">
        <h3>With the network off, it is the same app.</h3>
        <p class="off__p">The Local lane is the whole product, not a degraded one: dictation,
          cleanup, rewrite, translate, the agent. Weights on your disk, a runner on{' '}
          <span class="mono">127.0.0.1</span>, no account and no key.</p>
        <ul class="off__l">
          <li><b>No key</b><span>nothing to sign up for, nothing to revoke</span></li>
          <li><b>No request</b><span>no bytes leave the machine, so nothing can be logged</span></li>
        </ul>
        {/* WHAT IT COSTS ON DISK, AT BOTH ENDS, BECAUSE NOTHING HERE KNOWS
            WHICH END A READER LANDS ON.

            This was one figure -- the whole lane, deduped -- and it was a
            prediction dressed as a measurement. Nobody can be told which
            models somebody installs. A reader who only dictates fetches one
            speech model; a reader who runs all eight jobs locally holds four
            files. Printing only the second told the first that they owed seven
            gigabytes for a hundred-and-fifty-megabyte job.

            The spread is also the section's own argument restated: a profile
            decides each job separately, so there is no single download, and
            one number would contradict the paragraph at the top of the
            section.

            The ceiling is deduped by model in ../lib/catalogue -- eight jobs
            resolve to four files, two of them shared across jobs, so adding up
            the rows in the picker would report about triple. */}
        <dl class="off__f mono">
          <div>
            <dt>{LOCAL_INSTALL.floor}</dt>
            <dd>if you only dictate &mdash; one speech model, and that is a working install</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>{LOCAL_INSTALL.ceiling}</dt>
            <dd>if you run all eight jobs locally &mdash; {LOCAL_INSTALL.files} files, fetched once</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

    {/* The count, and nothing after it. This line used to close by saying that
        every default is one entry in a list and that keys stay in the secret
        store. Both are already on the surface above it: the credential line
        under every lane says where the key lives, and the profile block is
        four defaults being overridden. */}
    <p class="eng__foot mono rise" data-d="2">
      <b>{COUNTS.lanes}</b> lanes and <b>{COUNTS.models}</b> models across
      {' '}<b>{COUNTS.vendors}</b> vendors, in the catalogue both runtimes read.
      Every row above is that file's own default for that job. Last checked {COUNTS.read}.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
